## 1. Introduction
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting has been identified as one of the promising avenues for converting solar energy to clean and sustainable energy [1,2]. Nevertheless, owing to the low charge transfer efficiency and poor chemical stability, PEC water splitting performance is not satisfaction in application [3]. Therefore, developing robust photoanode materials with sufficient visible-light harvesting and efficient charge separation ability is still imperative [4,5]. Among available photoanode materials, monoclinic BVO is regarded as a desirable photoanode candidate owning to its appropriate bandgap $(2.4\mathrm{-}2.5\mathrm{eV})$ for light absorbing and favorable valence band (VB) edge for water oxidation [6,7]. However, it is still limited by poor charge separation and transfer efficiency [8] and sluggish water oxidation kinetics [9]. Therefore, some strategies, such as, element doping [10–12], oxygen vacancies etching [5,13], oxygen evolution co-catalysts (OEC) loading [14] and heterojunctions construction [15], have been developed to overcome these drawbacks.
Oxygen vacancies $(\boldsymbol{0}_{\mathrm{vac}})$ are a kind of classic defects and exist broadly in metal oxide surfaces due to relatively low formation energy [16]. Although the effect of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ on PEC water splitting is still vague and insufficiently understood, it is believed that the $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ could serve as a shallow donor in the energy band, inducing defect states to improve the charge conductivity and create active sites for water oxidation [17–19]. In addition, an appropriate $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ content is conducive to charge separation, by contrary, excessive $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ will induce the recombination of electron-hole pairs [20,21]. For example, Wang et al. directly converted a sulfur oxidation precursor to fabricate BVO photoanode with $0_{\mathrm{vac}},$ enhancing the charge separation efficiency of BVO [22]. Gong group adopted photoetching method to prepare surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ enriched BVO photoanode, improving the conductivity and creating photoinduced charge traps to boost charge separation [23]. Although $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ can facilitate charge separation efficiency of BVO, it still suffers from photocorrosion on account of the dissolution of vanadium ions during oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [24]. Therefore, coating of a protective layer on the surface of BVO photoanode is vital for stabilizing photoelectrodes [25].
In general, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is used as an inert layer to protect photoanode by isolating from electrolyte [26,27], whereas the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ protective layer may be not actively involved in the reaction process and even further block the access to active sites [28]. According to reports, decorating with oxygen evolution co-catalysts on BVO surface can not only accomplish the purpose of protecting the photoelectrode, but also can promote PEC water oxidation performance. For examples, Park et al. proved that the photoelectrodeposition of NiOOH thin layer loading on BVO photoanode could drive holes transport from BVO to the OEC film for efficient $0_{2}$ evolution and improve the stability [29]. Zhang group developed the NiFePhenolic network coating Mo-doped BVO photoanode, receiving a desirable photocurrent density and an excellent PEC stability [30]. Interestingly, MOFs, known as high porosity and adjustable chemical components, have achieved on applications of photocatalytic and electrocatalytic water splitting [31,32]. Xue et al. reported that Ni-MOFs is introduced into $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoarrays (Ni-MOFs $\left\vert\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}\right\vert$ ) as a OEC layer to promote photoinduced charge separation and enhance water oxidation performance [33].
Inspired by above-mentioned, we first create surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ enriched BVO photoanode via Ar-plasma treatment. Then NiFeMOFs grafting $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ (NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}8\mathsf{V}0\$ ) was fabricated by a facile hydrothermal method (Scheme 1). The NiFe-MOFs layer is in-situ grown on the $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO surface to form a uniform film. The presence of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ on BVO photoanode can boost charge separation to accelerate surface reaction, additionally, a thin NiFe-MOFs layer grafting on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ can not only protect the surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ of BVO, but also efficiently inhibits the photocorrosion. As a result, the resultant NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\mathrm{BVO}$ photoanode promotes chemical stability and achieves an outstanding photocurrent density $\cdot4.42\pm0$ $1\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Bismuth nitrate pentahydrate $(\mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $99.99\%$ ), potassium iodide (KI), p-Benzoquinone, vanadium acetylacetone oxygen $(\mathsf{V O}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}\cdot$ ), dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.5\%$ , nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}$ - $0{,}99.5\%)$ , caffeic acid (CA), terephthalic acid, nitric acid $\mathrm{(HNO_{3})}$ , ethyl alcohol, potassium hydroxide (KOH, $99.5\%$ , boric acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B O}_{3}$ , $99.8\%$ and sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $99.5\%$ ) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Fluorine-doped Tin Oxide (FTO) conductive glass was obtained from South China Xiangcheng Technology Co., Ltd, with a resistance of $6\Omega$ .
## 2.2. Synthesis of $O_{v a c}$ -BVO photoanodes
The nano-porous BVO electrodes were synthesized based on Choi’s method [34]. The resultant BVO electrodes were treated in a plasma reactor (Hefei Kejing Materials Technology Co., Ltd) for high-energy treatment with $\mathbf{Ar}_{2}$ sweeping. In order to successfully etch appropriate $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ the power should be adjusted to $2\:\mathrm{W}$ with pressure of 20 pa for reaction time of $120\ s$ under the rate of flow of 50 sccm (Fig. S1).
## 2.3. Synthesis of NiFe- $.0_{v a c}$ -BVO photoanodes
Terephthalic acid $\left(0.80\mathrm{\mmol}\right)^{\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\left(86.4~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ ${\mathfrak{g}}2.6{\mathrm{~mg}}{\mathrm{,~}}$ were dissolved in DMF $(40~\mathrm{ml})$ and stirred violently for 30 mins. The $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO electrode was immersed in $20~\mathrm{ml}$ ethanol dispersing $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of caffeic acid (CA) for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at 50 $\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ , and then put it into a $50\mathrm{ml}$ of autoclave containing above mixed solution. Furthermore, the autoclave was placed into an oven with $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Finally, the as-fabricated NiFe- $\scriptstyle\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}B\lor0$ electrode was rinsed with deionized water and dried in oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$
## 2.4. Material characterization
The crystal structures of the electrodes were examined by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D2 PHASER). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were carried out on JSM-IT500HR (Japan). UV– vis DRS were conducted on a spectrophotometer (UV-2600, Shimadzu). The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM, JEOLJEM 2100) was employed to study microstructure characteristics of the samples. The surface element and chemical states analysis were measured by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI-5000C ESCA). The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) signals of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ were obtained by using a Bruker A300.The photoluminescence (PL) spectra were examined on a fluorescence spectrometer (Labram-HR800) with laser excitation of $380~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 2.5. Photoelectrochemical measurements
Besides, the conversion from applied potentials to RHE was performed by the following Nernst equation:
$$
\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{R H E}}=0.197+0.0591*\mathtt{p H}+\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{A g/A g C l}}
$$
The solar simulator and a monochromator (CEL-IS151) were employed to measure incident photon to current efficiency (IPCE) in electrolyte of $0.5\mathrm{M}\mathrm{K}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ with different wavelength irradiation. The IPCE value was obtained by the equation [35]:
$$
\mathrm{IPCE}(\mathcal{Y}_{0})=\frac{1240\times\mathrm{J}\left(\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\right)}{\mathrm{P_{light}}(\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\times\lambda(\mathrm{nm})}\times100\%
$$
Where J is the photocurrent density measured at the specified photon wavelength $(\lambda)$ and $\mathrm{\bfP}_{\mathrm{light}}$ is incident light power density measured at $\lambda$ .
According to LSV curves, the following equation can calculate applied bias photon-to-current efficiency (ABPE):
$$
\mathsf{A B P E}(\mathcal{Y}_{0})=\frac{(1.23-\mathsf{V_{b i a s}})(\mathsf{V})\times\mathsf{J}\left(\mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}\right)}{\mathsf{P_{l i g h t}}(\mathsf{m W}/\mathsf{c m}^{-2})}\times100\%
$$
Where $\mathsf{V}_{\mathsf{b i a s}}$ refers to the applied bias, J is the photocurrent density, and $\mathsf{P}_{\mathrm{light}}$ is the incident light intensity ${\mathrm{.100~mW{\cdot}c m^{2}}}$ ).
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured on potentiostatic mode under AM 1.5G illumination. Furthermore, AC voltage amplitude of EIS spectra employed $10\mathrm{mV}$ at a 1.23 V vs. RHE DC bias. Mott-Schottky was carried out at the frequency of $1000~\mathrm{Hz}$ with the voltage range from 0.1 to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE.
## 2.6. The calculation of theoretical photocurrent
The energy of each photon was performed by the equation:
$$
\mathbf{E}_{\lambda}=\mathbf{h}\times\mathbf{C}/\lambda
$$
Where $\operatorname{E}_{\lambda}$ is energy of each photon (J), C and h are the speed of light $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{s}^{-1})$ and Planck’s constant (Js) and, respectively, and $\lambda$ refers to light wavelength (nm).
The photo flux (the number of photons) is converted from the equation:
$$
\mathsf{N}_{\uplambda}=\mathsf{P}_{\uplambda}/\mathsf{E}_{\uplambda}
$$
Where $\mathsf{P}_{\lambda}$ refers to the solar simulation spectral illumination $(\mathsf{W}{\cdot}\mathsf{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathsf{n m}^{-1},$ ) and $\mathsf{N}_{\uplambda}$ is photon flux $(\mathbf{m}^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{nm}^{-1}),$ .
The maximum theoretical photocurrent density is obtained by the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{max}}=\mathbf{e}\times\int_{300}^{510}\mathrm{N}_{\lambda}\mathrm{d}\lambda
$$
Where $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{max}}$ refers to the maximum theoretical photocurrent density $\left(\mathbf{mA\cdotcm^{-2}}\right).$ under the irradiation of simulated sunlight, and e represents the elementary charge and the value is $1.602\times10^{-19}($ . Based on the simulated solar spectrum and these equations, the theoretical maximum photocurrent of such BVO is $7.12~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure analysis
The structures of as-fabricated photoanodes were characterized by XRD. As depicted in Fig. S2, $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO, NiFe-BVO and NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}.$ - BVO show the unchanged monoclinic structure in comparison with BVO. The top-view and cross-sectional of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ photoanode SEM images (Fig. 1a) are similar to that of BVO (Fig. S3). After introducing a thin MOFs layer, the morphology of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ still not alters (Fig. 1b). Then the TEM measurement was performed. When compared to $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ (Fig. 1c), the TEM image of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO (Fig. 1d) shows that a thin NiFe-MOFs layer $(7.1~\mathrm{nm})$ is uniformly decorated on the surface of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-\mathrm{BVO}}}$ with the lattice spacing $0.286\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1e). The corresponding element mapping images (Fig. 1f) demonstrate the fairly well-distributed Ni and Fe elements on the surface of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO, revealing that the atomic ratio of Ni and Fe is 2.7:3.0 (Fig. S4a, S4b). To further confirm the NiFe-MOFs grafting on the surface of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ , FTIR spectra (Fig. S5) and XRD patterns of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO with various crystallization time (Fig. S6) were characterized. The peaks at 1583 and $1380\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are indexed to stretching vibrations of carboxylate groups in NiFe-MOFs ligand [36]. The XRD patterns of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO after crystallization of $40\mathrm{~h~}$ exhibit a diffraction peak at $8.9^{\circ}$ , matching the (200) crystal plane of NiFe-MOFs [37].
In order to figure out surface state and electronic structures of NiFe- $\scriptstyle.0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-\mathrm{BVO}}}$ photoanodes, XPS was performed. Compared to BVO, the Bi 4f and $\textsf{V}2\mathsf{p}$ (Fig. 2a and Fig. 2b) of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ shift to the lower binding energy, which is due to the existence of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ leading to the reduction of coordination number on $\mathsf{B i}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{V}^{5+}$ ions [38]. From Fig. 2c, the O 1 s spectra can be fitted into three peaks, lattice oxygen $(0_{\mathrm{L}})$ , hydroxyl groups bonded to the metal cations in the oxygen deficient region $(0_{\mathsf{V}})$ and chemisorbed or dissociated oxygen species from water molecules $(0_{\mathsf{C}})$ [22]. The $0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}$ BVO exhibits much higher $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ content than that of BVO, further illustrating the generation of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ after plasma treatment. However, the Bi 4f and V 2p of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO express a remarkable shift to higher binding energy than $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ , suggesting that holes transfer from $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ to NiFe-MOF [28]. As depicted in Fig. 2d, the fitting peaks at 855.7 and $873.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ are defined as the Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , respectively, and the peaks at 880.5 and $860.6\mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to satellite peaks [39]. The binding energies of Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ (Fig. 2e) at 711.6 and $725.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are identified as Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , respectively, and similarly located at 729.8 and $715.9\mathrm{eV}$ peaks to satellite peaks [40]. To further testify the formation of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ , EPR was investigated [41]. The stronger EPR signal for $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ than BVO indicates more $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ are generated on BVO after Ar-plasma treatment and the stronger EPR signal for NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO than $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO may be due to the existence of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ in NiFe-MOFs thin layer.
## 3.2. Photoelectrochemical performances
PEC water splitting property of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO was evaluated in 0.5 M ${\bf K}_{3}{\bf B}{\sf O}_{3}$ electrolyte $\mathrm{\Phi}_{\mathrm{\tiny{pH}}}=9.3\$ ) under AM 1.5G illumination $(100\mathrm{\mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}).$ ). As depicted in Fig. 3a, the photocurrent density of BVO photoanode is $1.2\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23{\mathrm{V}}$ vs. RHE and the value is relatively low due to sluggish water oxidation kinetics. After etching $0_{\mathrm{vac}},$ the photocurrent density of $0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-\mathrm{BVO}}}$ reaches $1.9\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is 1.6-time higher than that of BVO. Especially, 1 $\scriptstyle\mathtt{J i F e-O}_{\mathrm{vac}}-\mathrm{BVO}$ exhibits an outstanding photocurrent density of 4. $42\pm0.1\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is obviously higher than that of NiFeBVO $(3.1\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ and BVO. Note that NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode exhibits the lowest onset potential, indicating that grafting of a thin NiFe-MOFs layer and the generation of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ can effectively improve water oxidation kinetics [42]. What’s more, the chopped photocurrent density under interval illumination suggests that water oxidation is driven by photogenerated charge carriers (Fig. S7). Then ABPE was obtained according to the result of LSV curves (Fig. 2b). The maximal value of ABPE for NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}$ BVO can reach $1.67\%$ , higher than that of NiFe-BVO $(1.25\%)$ , $0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}$ BVO $(0.32\%)$ and BVO $(0.17\%)$ . Moreover, IPCE was conducted at $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE with various wavelength irradiance. As seen in Fig. 3c and Fig. S8, the IPCE of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode is much higher than that of NiFe-BVO, $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ and BVO photoanodes. To analyze the separation ability of photogenerated electrons and holes, photocurrent density for chopping irradiation was conducted at $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE (Fig. 3d). NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}B{\lor}0$ exhibits the strongest transient photocurrent intensity. Furthermore, the recombination behavior of photoexcited charges was further investigated by the transient time constant $(\uptau)$ which was calculated by the following equation [43]:
$$
D=(I(t)-I(s t))/(I(i n)-I(s t))
$$
Where $I(t)$ is the current density at a time t, I in is the current density at $\mathbf{t}=0$ and I st is the steady state current density. The $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ is defined as the time at which ln $\mathsf{D}=-1$ [44]. It can be seen that the s of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode reaches 6.1 s, much higher than that of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ (3.1 s) and BVO (2.0 s) (Fig. 3e), indicating the prolonged decay of photogenerated charge carriers. Subsequently, EIS was employed to elucidate electron transfer resistance (Rct) of interface between photoelectrode and electrolyte [45]. As shown in Fig. 3f, the NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode represents the fastest charge transfer compared with the rest of photoanodes (Table S1). Mott-Schottky curves were investigated in Fig. S9, the flat-band potential of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode is more negative than $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ and BVO photoanodes, suggesting the more negative potential for hydrogen evolution reaction [30].
## 3.3. Spectrum and water oxidation kinetics
PL spectra were employed to assess the behaviors of charge carrier separation and transport [46]. As Fig. 4a, the strong PL peak of BVO signifies the relatively high recombination rate of photogenerated electrons and holes. After etching $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ and further grafting NiFe-MOFs, the electron-hole recombination rate is distinctly suppressed. The peak at $515\mathrm{nm}$ is near the light absorption band edge of BVO $(510\ \mathrm{nm})$ (Fig. 4d), which is ascribed to irradiative recombination of the electron in V 3d band and the hole in O 2p band [47], the peak at $487~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is related to $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ in FTO substrates (Fig. S10). Additionally, to research the surface hole-transfer behavior, the transient-state surface photovoltage (TS-SPV) responses were conducted. As seen in Fig. 4b, the NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode possesses the highest photovoltages intensity and the longest-lived holes, suggesting that photoinduced holes reaching photoanode surface are largely boosted and the recombination of electron-hole pairs is greatly suppressed, respectively [48,49]. LSV plots in the dark for BVO, $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ and NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\mathrm{BVO}$ photoanodes were measured to study the boosted water oxidation activities (Fig. S11a). Obviously, the $\mathsf{N i F e-O_{v a c}}$ -BVO photoanode exhibits a lower overpotential and steeper current density, revealing its outstanding electrocatalytic activity [9]. According to LSV curves under dark condition, the Tafel slope was calculated to investigate the water oxidation kinetics. As shown in Fig. R6, the $0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-\mathrm{BVO}}}$ exhibits a decreased Tafel slope after coating a NiFeMOF layer, indicating an enhanced oxygen evolution kinetics. The open-circuit potential (OCP) is tested in Fig. 4c, NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO and exhibit higher photovoltage than that of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ and BVO, demonstrating greater driving force to promote charge separation [22]. To further understand the water oxidation kinetics, the Jabs was obtained by the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{J_{abs}=J_{m a x}\times L H E}
$$
Where $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{max}}$ refers to the maximum theoretical photocurrent $(\mathrm{m}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ under the irradiation of simulated sunlight, LHE is light harvesting efficiency and $\mathsf{J a b s}$ is photocurrent $\left(\mathbf{mA\cdotcm^{-2}}\right.$ ) when light harvesting efficiency is $100\%$ .
$$
\boldsymbol\upeta_{\mathrm{sep}}=\mathrm{J_{HS}/J}_{a b s}
$$
Where $\boldsymbol\upeta_{\mathsf{s e p}}$ is charge separation efficiency and $\mathrm{J_{HS}}$ is photocurrent density $\left(\mathbf{mA\cdotcm^{-2}}\right),$ measured in containing hole scavenger electrolyte. As seen in UV–vis absorbance spectra, the light absorption band edges of BVO, $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ and NiFe- $\scriptstyle.0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}B\lor0$ photoanodes are at $510~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 4d). Hence, $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{max}}$ and LHE can be calculated on the basis of the absorption range of BVO $300\sim510\mathrm{nm}$ . The value of LHE and $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{max}}$ are calculated and show in Fig. S12, and the $\mathrm{J_{HS}}$ is measured in 0.5 M $\mathsf{K}_{3}\mathsf{B O}_{3}$ electrolyte containing 0.5 M ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{3}$ as hole scavenger [50] (Fig. S13). As depicted in Fig. 4e, the presence of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ on BVO evidently improves charge separation efficiency than that of BVO photoanode, which illustrates that $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ generate coordinatively unsaturated on the surface of BVO, accelerating surface reaction [51]. As seen in Fig. S14, the LSV curves for front and rear illumination of BVO photoanode are performed. It obviously seems that the photocurrent density of BVO photoanode irradiated on the rear is higher than front illumination, which is associated with poor electron transport properties of the BVO photoanode [52].To quantify the charge transfer efficiency $(\upeta_{\mathrm{tran}})$ , it was calculated by the equation:
$$
\eta_{\mathrm{tran}}=\mathrm{J_{Ph}/J_{H S}}
$$
Where $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{Ph}}$ refers to photocurrent density $\left(\mathbf{mA\cdotcm^{-2}}\right.$ performed in 0.5 M ${\bf K}_{3}{\bf B}{\sf O}_{3}$ electrolyte. Because of the photogenerated holes transferring from $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ to NiFe-MOFs thin layer, the $\boldsymbol\upeta_{\mathrm{tran}}$ of NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\mathrm{BVO}$ reaches $83.0\%$ by up to 2.4-time higher than that of BVO (Fig. 4f), implying the synergism between $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ and NiFeMOFs co-catalysts.
## 3.4. Photoelectrochemical water oxidation of NiFe- $O_{\nu a c}$ -BVO photoanode
The hydrophilicity of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ photoanode was measured by contact angle (CA). The $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ and BVO possess the similar CA (Fig. 5a and Fig. S15), indicating the presence of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ has little effect on hydrophilicity. However, after grafting a thin MOFs layer, the contact angle reduces to $11.22^{\circ}$ and the wettability is enormously increased (Fig. 5b). The improved hydrophilicity can accelerate $0_{2}$ evolution from electrode surface, enhancing the efficiency of OER. The comparison of OER performance of BVO based photoanodes (Table S2) indicates that NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO exhibits excellent PEC performance. The stability measurement demonstrates that NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ - BVO photoanode can stably exist, efficiently inhibiting the photocorrosion of BVO (Fig. 5c). Besides, the amounts of the evolved gases were measured by a gas chromatography (Fig. 5d). The produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ are close to 2:1 and their linear fitting diagram is almost consistent with the theoretical gas amounts calculated by the current density. The XRD, SEM, TEM and EDS spectra after stability test (Fig. S16-S18) further indicate that NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode possesses excellent stability. To explore the electronic structure change of NiFe- $\cdot0_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\mathrm{BVO}$ photoanode, XPS spectra was performed. The slight change of $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ content after PEC measurement illustrates that the NiFe-MOFs co-catalyst layer can protect the surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ of BVO (Fig. S19c). To further prove it, the EPR of NiFe- $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol}{D}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{BVO}}$ before and after test were measured. From Fig. S20, it can be seen that the EPR signal of NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO after test shows a slight decrease, indicating the surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ of BVO are well protected. The $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ peak after OER at $859.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ reveals a proportion of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ is oxidized to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ by holes migrating from $0_{\mathrm{vac}}.$ BVO (Fig. 5e) [53]. The barely shift of Fe, Bi and $\mathsf{v}$ (Fig. 5f and Fig. S19a-b) indicates that surface chemical environment present neglected change after stability test. Therefore, the series of characterizations remarkably demonstrate NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode possesses the high durability and OER performance.
tion reaction, but also protect the electrode to extend lifetime of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{vac}^{-}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ . More specifically, the NiFe-MOFs layer provides active sites for OER, meanwhile, it protects the surface $0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ of BVO and suppresses the photocorrosion. As a result, the NiFe- $.0_{\mathrm{vac}}$ -BVO photoanode achieves an excellent photocurrent density $(4.42\pm0.1\mathrm{~m~}$ $\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ ) and high stability. A new insight is proposed by this work to improve PEC water splitting activity and construct highly efficient and durable OER catalysts.
